    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d986">

                <pb facs="1039" n="753" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg753"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/3718</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Italian Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Macchi di Cellere</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-02">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 2, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: It will no
                    doubt have occurred to you that, since Germany and Austria-Hungary announced
                    their intention to sink armed merchantmen at sight on and after March 1 <pb
                        facs="1040" n="754" xml:id="pg754"/> ultimo, the mere sighting of a
                    submarine by an armed merchantman amounts to an attack, as armed merchantmen
                    have been notified that they will be fired upon without warning.</p>
                <p>Consequent to these new conditions, the instructions issued up to now to the
                    captains of Italian armed merchantmen “not to use their guns to attack a
                    submarine whilst being warned,” expose such ships to wanton destruction whilst
                    they are waiting for a warning which will no longer be given them by enemy
                    submarines.</p>
                <p>I, therefore, venture to suggest that the sentence “and will not be used to
                    attack a submarine whilst being warned” be omitted in the declarations which
                    this Embassy issues for Italian armed merchantmen visiting American ports.</p>
                <p>I am confident that you will find my request perfectly justified and whilst
                    awaiting the favor of a reply, I beg to remain [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Macchi di Cellere</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
